Theorem CH-105: monotonicity engine bootstrap (TF-3 Construct monotonicity)

  Preconditions:
    - the Access x Consumption 2-dim sub-lattice per Annex E §AIMS §3.1 + §AIMS §3.2
    - Access carrier: Borrowed less than Owned (height 1)
    - Consumption carrier: Dead less than Linear less than Affine
      less than Unrestricted (height 3)
    - the partial order on the sub-lattice is componentwise
      less-than-or-equal per L-4
    - TF_construct is a synthetic transfer function modeling
      Annex E §AIMS TF-3 Construct restricted to the
      Access x Consumption sub-lattice; TF_construct s returns
      (Owned, Linear) for every input s on the sub-lattice (the
      Construct instruction writes FRESH outputs independent of
      the input state per TF-3)
    - monotonicity engine consumes per-pair (a, b) finite checks
      of TF(a) less-than-or-equal-to TF(b) per the foundational-axiom policy
      sec-Per-Engine-Constructive-Proof-Shape

  Soundness property:
    Forall a, b in (Access x Consumption) with a less-than-or-equal-to b.
      TF_construct a less-than-or-equal-to TF_construct b;
      i.e. TF_construct is monotone on the
        Access x Consumption sub-lattice (L-6 restricted form).

  Proof obligation:
    Per-pair finite enumeration over the 8 states of the
    Access x Consumption sub-lattice; for each ordered pair (a, b)
    with a less-than-or-equal-to b, exhibit TF_construct on both
    and verify the order is preserved.

      Setup:
        TF_construct collapses every state to (Owned, Linear) per
        the TF-3 Construct rule (FRESH output independent of
        input). Therefore TF_construct a = (Owned, Linear) and
        TF_construct b = (Owned, Linear) for every a and b.
        (Owned, Linear) is reflexively less-than-or-equal-to
        itself; therefore TF_construct a
        less-than-or-equal-to TF_construct b is trivially true.

      Per-ordered-pair coverage (8 states; 28 ordered pairs with
      a strictly-less-than b plus 8 reflexive a = b cases yield
      36 ordered pairs):
        For each (a, b) where a less-than-or-equal-to b:
          TF_construct a = (Owned, Linear);
          TF_construct b = (Owned, Linear);
          (Owned, Linear) less-than-or-equal-to (Owned, Linear)
          holds reflexively.
        Therefore the monotonicity property holds for every
        ordered pair on the sub-lattice.

    Closed-world coverage:
      Every input state on the 8-state sub-lattice maps to the
      same FRESH output (Owned, Linear); the monotonicity property
      is satisfied vacuously because the output is constant under
      the input partial order. This is the canonical monotone
      behavior for the Construct transfer function: it produces
      FRESH outputs whose ordering is determined by the constant
      output, not by the input ordering.

    Lattice validation:
      L-6 (transfer functions are monotone) is restated for the
      sub-lattice and discharged by the constant-output argument
      above. The restricted form composes monotonically with L-6
      on the full 7-dim lattice when the excluded dimensions
      (Cardinality, Uniqueness, Locality, Shape, Effect) are at
      their TF-3 FRESH values: Cardinality = Once, Uniqueness =
      Unique, Locality = BlockLocal, Shape = shape_from_ctor,
      Effect = {may_alloc=true}. These are also constant for every
      input to TF-3 Construct.

    Engines dispatched:
      monotonicity (per-pair finite checks; constant-output
                    argument; L-6 restricted-form discharge)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: 01A bootstrap discharged via §01A Implementation Items (engine constructive verification + Lean 4 cross-validation)
